		id: 'verse-1',
		heading: 'Verse 1: “My Father is my shepherd”',
		bodyHtml: `<p>The psalm is a song of David, and David knew sheep. He opens with the whole claim in one line: &ldquo;My Father is my shepherd; I lack nothing.&rdquo; (Psalm 23:1). The Hebrew verb <em>r&#257;&lsquo;&#257;h</em> (Strong&rsquo;s H7462) means to tend a flock, and to pasture it, feed it and keep it. A shepherd does not stand over sheep at a distance. He is with them, and everything they have comes from his care.</p><p>That is why &ldquo;I lack nothing&rdquo; is not a boast about having plenty. It is a statement about whose flock he belongs to. Father&rsquo;s Heart Bible lets the line say what the Hebrew pictures: the Shepherd is the Father, and the one who sings is a child of His.</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'verses-2-3',
		heading: 'Verses 2-3: rest, water and a restored soul',
		bodyHtml: `<p>&ldquo;In green pastures he lets me lie down; beside quiet waters he leads me.&rdquo; (Psalm 23:2). Sheep only lie down when they are unafraid and fed, so the picture is a flock at rest, led to the water instead of driven to it. Then: &ldquo;He restores my soul; he guides me in paths of righteousness for the sake of his name.&rdquo; (Psalm 23:3).</p><p>Two things are said at once. Our Father restores the weary, and He guides them along right paths. And the reason He does it is His own name: He leads this way because of who He is, not because the sheep have earned it. Compare Ezekiel 34:15, where our Father says, &ldquo;I myself will tend my flock and have them lie down, declares the Sovereign Father.&rdquo; The psalm and the prophet describe the same Shepherd.</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'verse-4',
		heading: 'Verse 4: the valley of the shadow of death',
		bodyHtml: `<p>The mood turns in the middle of the psalm, and so does the pronoun. Until now David spoke <em>about</em> the Shepherd (&ldquo;he leads me&rdquo;); here he speaks <em>to</em> Him: &ldquo;Even though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death, I will fear no evil, for you are with me; your rod and your staff comfort me.&rdquo; (Psalm 23:4). The fear is not removed by the valley being easy. It is removed by the one who is walking beside him.</p><p>The Hebrew behind &ldquo;shadow of death&rdquo; is <em>tsalm&#257;wet</em>, which many scholars read as &ldquo;deep darkness.&rdquo; Either way the valley is real, and the verse says you walk <em>through</em> it, not into it and stop. The rod defends the flock and the staff guides it, which is why both bring comfort. Jesus uses the same shepherd picture of Himself: &ldquo;My sheep listen to my voice; I know them, and they follow me.&rdquo; (John 10:27).</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'verse-5',
		heading: 'Verse 5: a table in front of my enemies',
		bodyHtml: `<p>&ldquo;You prepare a table before me in the presence of my enemies; you anoint my head with oil; my cup overflows.&rdquo; (Psalm 23:5). The scene changes from field to feast. The enemies have not gone away; they are in the room and can only watch. Anointing the head with oil was what a host did for an honored guest, and an overflowing cup means more than enough.</p><p>So the verse is not a promise of a life without enemies. It is a promise that our Father sets the table, and that His guest is safe at it.</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'verse-6',
		heading: 'Verse 6: goodness pursues me, and I live in my Father’s house',
		bodyHtml: `<p>&ldquo;Surely goodness and unfailing love will pursue me all the days of my life, and I will live in the house of my Father forever.&rdquo; (Psalm 23:6). &ldquo;Unfailing love&rdquo; is the Hebrew <em>chesed</em> (H2617), His covenant faithfulness, and &ldquo;pursue&rdquo; is <em>r&#257;daph</em> (H7291), the word used for chasing someone down. David pictures goodness and love running after him, not him struggling to keep up with them.</p><p>The psalm ends where it was heading all along: home. In this Bible the last words are &ldquo;the house of my Father forever,&rdquo; and Jesus carries the same hope into the upper room: &ldquo;In my Father&rsquo;s house are many rooms.&rdquo; (John 14:2).</p>`,
	},
];

